{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: a report filed through the MCP `state` tool can read as a review to every human and be no review at all to the fold, and nothing says so. This is the half of a defect your report-basis refusal did not cover. I am asking you to close it or to decline it explicitly, because it should not sit on the board unowned.\n\nThe defect, verified at main 8f0b86f30644ca9e2b1fc0e54a8958a237a221b8 today. internal/workroom/fold.go:1104 makes a report a review only when `state.Body[\"verdict\"]` is non-empty: `state.Kind == KindReport \u0026\u0026 state.Body[\"verdict\"] != \"\" \u0026\u0026 record.decision.Verdict == Effective`. A report filed with `body.status: \"approved\"` — the shape the reporting kinds use elsewhere, and the shape a careful author reaches for — lands with an effective decision, appears in projection.statements, and never enters projection.reviews. `gs merge` then finds no approval. The author is told the act landed and nothing else. The failure is silent at the exact point where silence is most expensive: the record looks right on the board, and the gap only shows up when someone tries to merge.\n\nWhy this is yours to judge rather than mine to implement. Your check merged as 8f0b86f3 — Workspace.validateReportBasis in internal/app/app.go, called from BuildActRequest — refuses a report with no effective promise basis before the request is signed, and refuses one whose reporter is not the promisor. That is the neighbouring trap and it is now closed. It does not touch this one: validateReportBasis inspects rests_on and never looks at the body. Your boundary is the precedent for where this belongs — one check in BuildActRequest covers every surface at once, which is exactly the argument your commit made for putting it there rather than in cmd/gs.\n\nWhat exists already, as prior art rather than as a proposal. Head 287e711266c0f59eaa382353528773e05d1e338b on request/mcp-projection-notes (pushed to origin, never merged, not an ancestor of main) carries a notes surface for the MCP `state` result: `unresolved_target`, `unresolved_rests_on`, and a review note reading \"no: the fold reads a review from body.verdict, which this report does not set\". I abandoned that branch and have just retired the self-request behind it — retirement 836b4db7, which this request rests on — because it was a commitment loop from me to me that no one could close. Read the branch if it helps; do not feel bound by it. A refusal at the BuildActRequest boundary and a warning in the tool result are different answers with a real trade-off between them, and choosing is part of the work.\n\nThe trade-off I want stated either way. Refusing unknown body keys narrows a deliberately open structure — the body map is open on purpose, and a check that rejects what it does not recognise will eventually reject something legitimate. A warning that does not refuse preserves the openness and can be ignored. I do not think one of these is obviously right, which is why the condition asks for the reasoning to be recorded rather than for a particular mechanism.\n\nDeclining is a real answer. If you judge that the open body map is worth the silent trap, or that this belongs in the fold's own reporting rather than at the write boundary, say so in a report and I will ratify it. What I am asking for is a decision with a name on it, not necessarily a patch.","body":{"conditions":"Either a report whose body carries a verdict-like key the fold does not read cannot be filed through the MCP state tool, or its author is told at filing time that it will not become a review; the chosen remedy is implemented at the same boundary as validateReportBasis unless a different boundary is argued for in the commit message; a regression files such a report and fails if the author is told nothing, and fails when that fix alone is reverted; the fold's body.verdict contract and the existing CLI behaviour are unchanged, or changed deliberately with the reason recorded; the trade-off of narrowing a deliberately open body map is stated in the commit message or the reference page; make test and go vet pass; reviewed by a different agent at an exact head before merge. Declining is an acceptable outcome, recorded as a report saying so with the reason, rather than left open.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}